tickets.body:
Semantic actions: Light, Heavy (press, hold, release), Guard, Dodge, Jump, Interact, WeaponCycle/Selector, Sprint, AbilityLayer plus Slot1-4, AimConfirm and AimCancel. Default bindings for both devices follow the rationale. Finalize the keyboard/mouse keys against the native key list so nothing the game still uses is lost. Write both tables to docs/combat/controls.md.

Pure arbiter in Core:
- context priority: menus > native order mode > ability aiming > ability layer > basic attacks;
- chord consumption (LT+B never dodges; modifier+key never fires the base action);
- one press is one action;
- a small buffer (about 150 ms) for the next combo step;
- everything held or buffered is dropped on pause, menu, mount change, death or device switch.

Game side:
- read the combat GameKeyContext;
- add a Harmony patch on MissionMainAgentController (TaleWorlds.MountAndBlade.View.dll) so the native attack/block doesn't also fire for actions the layer owns;
- leave native orders, interaction, inventory and menus with the game;
- expose a fake input source the probe can drive for either device.

Checks (lean): compiles; a few Core assertions cover chord consumption, priority and drop-on-transition. No game launch.

AbilityDefinition JSON fields:
- id, namespaced as pack.ability;
- name and description localization keys, and an icon;
- foot and/or mounted;
- requirements: weapon class or usage, shield, ammo, couch;
- unlock: a native skill plus a threshold;
- stamina cost and cooldown;
- kind: instant, hold/charge, aimed-confirm, or counter-after-guard;
- recipe steps from a small fixed vocabulary: play a native action, a native attack with damage/stagger multipliers (through Sprint 1's hit patch), move (reusing the dodge mover), aim-confirm, and a timed buff.

The loader scans ModuleData/CombatAbilities/*.json in every enabled module, this one included, so first-party abilities take the same path as anyone else's. An invalid file is skipped with a log line naming the file and field. For duplicate IDs, the first module in load order wins and the duplicate is logged.

The executor checks requirements, stamina and cooldown on press and again at commit. Cancelling before commit refunds. Same behaviour on both devices.

Checks (lean): compiles; assertions for loading valid and invalid samples and the press-commit-cooldown flow. No game launch.

One Gauntlet screen, reachable from the campaign (character screen or pause menu). Add a mission pause entry only if the native flow allows it cleanly; otherwise record that it is campaign-only.

Layout:
- Foot/Mounted tabs with four slot cards each;
- an ability list with filters (usable / all / locked);
- a detail panel: cost, cooldown, requirements, unlock reason, source pack;
- assign, clear and swap, with Apply/Cancel.

Input: full gamepad navigation (A selects, B goes back, bumpers switch tabs) and full mouse/keyboard use. Button and key names come from the current bindings. No presets. All strings use localization keys.

Checks (lean): compiles; view-model assertions for assign, swap, clear and Apply/Cancel. No game launch.

Show:
- a stamina bar;
- guard and charge cues;
- the Foot/Mounted badge;
- per-family ranged prompts;
- an ability panel with four slots showing the bound button or key, cost, cooldown and disabled reason, expanded while the ability layer is held;
- confirm/cancel hints for aimed abilities.

The native health, ammo and order HUD stays visible. Hit feedback (small screen shake, rumble on gamepad) is local and can be switched off in settings.

Checks (lean): compiles. No game launch.

One MCM page, global per user:
- stick deadzones and sensitivity;
- hold or toggle for guard, aim and the ability layer;
- trigger threshold;
- rumble and screen shake on/off;
- HUD scale.

Remapping stays in the native Controls screen through the GameKeyContext; the MCM page says so.

Checks (lean): compiles; settings reach the input layer. No game launch.

A separate module, samples/abilities/ExampleAnimatedPack, containing:
- one foot ability and one mounted ability, each using an original attack animation authored in Blender on the Bannerlord skeleton and registered through the module's own action_sets.xml and action_types.xml;
- one tiny C# effect using IAbilityEffect;
- source files and a license for the art.

docs/combat/modding/custom-animations.md gives the exact tool steps: Modding Kit import, resource registration, and the restart needed. Add pack steps to the combat probe: assign and use both abilities, and hit and get blocked by a native AI.

Checks (lean): the pack validates; compiles; probe preflight passes. No game launch.

One in-game probe that every sprint's live round uses. Reuse the repo's autorun probe pattern (src/MNBL.ProceduralWorld/Automation/AutorunHost.cs, tools/Start-OwnedProbe.ps1, tools/Invoke-InGameProbe.ps1), copied into the combat module rather than depending on ProceduralWorld.

The probe:
- starts a small custom-battle-style mission (player versus a few native troops);
- drives the input layer's fake source as gamepad, then as keyboard/mouse;
- records outcomes from native events (hits, blocks, damage, stamina, positions) into one receipt JSON plus a few screenshots;
- makes every step fail-soft: record the result and continue.

Sprint 1 steps:
- each melee class does light, heavy, charged and mixed chains against a standing target and against a guarding AI;
- guard and timed guard against AI attacks;
- dodge in the open, into a wall, on a slope and through a crowd;
- ability-layer chords never fire base actions;
- shield loss and weapon switch mid-chain.

The runner refuses while any Bannerlord*/Watchdog process exists. Later sprints add their steps to this probe.

Checks (lean): compiles; preflight mode passes against the real install without launching.

The epic's single review: one pass over the Sprint 1 diff, looking only for what would waste a live run or break the shared install:
- unhandled exceptions on the mission thread;
- Harmony patches that could fire outside combat missions or for non-player agents;
- input that can stick (held or buffered actions surviving a transition);
- missing teardown when the mission ends;
- runner restore and foreign-session paths.

Fix findings directly. Report a short findings list only: no receipts, no re-review rounds. Later sprints have no review ticket.

Add Sprint 2's steps to the combat probe, fail-soft, run once on gamepad input and once on keyboard/mouse input:
- load a campaign save with a hero, open Combat Arts by input, assign both pages and Apply;
- fight on foot (melee, bow, throwing) and mounted (melee, couch, bow), using each starter ability once;
- mount, dismount and horse death while ability input is held;
- save, reload and check the slots;
- rename a test ability file so its ID goes missing: the slot shows unavailable; restore the file and the ability comes back.

Keep a short smoke of Sprint 1's steps.

Checks (lean): compiles; preflight passes without launching.

Add Sprint 3's steps to the combat probe, fail-soft:
- one short fight per available mission type from docs/combat/mission-types.md (ship deck only if War Sails is enabled), including the hand-back cases;
- each catalog ability used once;
- install from the clean package with the dev deployment removed;
- one FPS reading on an existing large-battle scenario with the mod on and off, same settings;
- load a save made with the mod while the mod is disabled.

Run the steps on both devices where input matters. Keep the Sprint 1 and 2 smoke.

Checks (lean): compiles; preflight passes without launching.
